Problem Understanding
What the problem is asking: Now we have to build upon the basic program created in L1 by turning it into a proper battle simulator with duels lasting until one of the benders faints. Type advantages, turn order, critical hits and more features are added making the program more nuanced.

Key concepts involved: Like last time, classes and constructors are used but what's new here is the implementation of type matchups using arrays and critical hits at random using the rand() function.

My approach: 
For type matchups, instead of defining each type matchup separately, I have simply created a list having types in such an order that the type to the right is stronger against the type that comes before and it finally loops back. To accomplish this easily, I have defined an "element_number" for each type which is nothing but the index of the type in list:
0 -> Fire; 1 -> Water; 2 -> Earth; 3 -> Air
I would like to add that my method woeks only because the type matchups are defined such that they form a closed loop(square) with no diagonals or anything. If that were the case, I would have had define a class of elements and proceed.
For implementing the element of chance in the form of critical hits (and turn order when speeds are equal) I have used rand(), srand(), and time() in a standard manner.
Battles can run both in auto mode and in manual mode; decided when the duel function is called.

Conceptual Learning
New Concepts I Discovered
[Concept Name]: Getting random numbers in C++; it is very useful as a multitude of applications require random sequences of numbers for certain features.
[Another Concept]: I learnt how commonly time() is used to generate random numbers as rand() truly is not random. Also the technique of using modulo(%) to get the random number and move input into range deserves a honorary mention.
How I Applied These Concepts
I applied these concepts in a standard manner where time() is used to make rand() work more like a true random number generator.

Real-World Connections
This problem is the base of all turned-based RPG battle like that in Pokemon. Besides that random numbers are used everywhere especially in gaming (called RNG). For example, in minecraft when you kill a mob the loot it drops depends on chance, implemented using random numbers. Another example could be matchmaking, where players of similar skillsets are matched at random. For a more practical example, we maybe can consider OTPs.
